WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.760
- Welcome back to another
episode of the Addy Hour.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.310
As always, I'm just honored to be able

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.210
to host these conversations.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:14.130
As you'll notice in these
recent conversations,

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.140
we're bringing in some
different types of topics,

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.850
wanna kind of keep things
fresh and relevant,

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.050
but also just get at the heart

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.970
of really some important issues.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.140
So today we're jumping in

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.740
and talking about Black bodies
and race in art and society,

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.210
and I have two wonderful guests

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.670
who I think you all are really
gonna enjoy hearing from.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.680
They already had some
good chemistry together,

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.060
off camera, offline.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.440
So I'm actually really curious

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.290
to see where this
conversation is gonna go today

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.880
with this important topic.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.230
But by way of introduction,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.320
gonna go ahead first and
introduce Daniel Swann.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:44.670
Daniel is an assistant professor

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.610
of sociology at Goucher College.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.950
He completed his doctorate in sociology

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.230
at the University of
Maryland and then, or also,

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:55.320
before that earned his
undergrad degree at Rutgers.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.140
He's taught a whole host of
courses, including courses on

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.200
comparative social media, Generation Z,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.750
race and ethnic relations,
social movements,

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:06.540
wealth, power, and prestige,
and also data analytics.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.400
So a wide range of topics.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.210
He also has academic interest
in race, atheism and religion,

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.690
and political sociology
and social psychology.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:16.920
He's the author of some books,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.630
has also contributed
to some book chapters.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.570
One book I wanted to highlight
is his qualitative study of

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.670
Black atheists, "Don't Tell
Me You're One of Those"

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.740
that was published in 2020.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.750
In addition to the work
that he's been doing,

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.790
he also taught at University Park Maryland

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.670
before joining where he is now.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.110
So, Danny, just wanted
to welcome you again

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.030
to the Addy Hour podcast.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:37.582
- Awesome. It's great to be here.

00:01:37.582 --> 00:01:39.166
Thanks for having me.

00:01:39.166 --> 00:01:40.230
- And yeah.
- Of course.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.750
- Chemistry, you know, just makes me think

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.880
that things are gonna
gonna go really well, so.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.880
- Yes, (chuckling) I
anticipate that as well.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.790
My second guest I would like
to introduce is Imo Imeh,

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:54.930
who is a visual artist and a
scholar of African diaspora art

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.660
whose work considers historical
and philosophical issues

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.750
around the black body
and cultural identity.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.000
His work has been exhibited
in numerous venues

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.310
across the Northeast and the US.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.710
His work is also represented
in the Petrucci Foundation

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.570
Collection of African-American Art,

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:11.550
also in the museum collection

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.890
of the University of
Massachusetts Amherst.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.990
His work's been featured on PBS News Hour,

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:19.200
New England Public Media,
and other places as well.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.940
And this year in 2023,

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.140
he has actually partnered

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.960
with the Medical Journal
of Biological Psychiatry

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.080
to have his work appear on
the cover of six issues.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.070
And that's something that I've been able

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.900
to be involved in, as well.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.450
So grateful for the work
he's doing on many fronts.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.850
Imo, as someone who actually graduated

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.550
from Columbia University in 2022.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.410
Oh my goodness, that'd be nice.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.630
2002. (chuckling)

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.780
And then earned his PhD in
history of African-American art

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.030
at Yale, where we actually
overlapped as a grad student.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:49.590
So Imo, thanks so much for being here

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.450
on the Addy Hour Podcast.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:52.320
- Thank you so much.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.430
Wonderful to be with you, Nii,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.890
and wonderful to be with you, Daniel.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.750
- Excellent. I'm not trying
to make you too young.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.930
That was just a slip of the
song at that point. (chuckling)

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.160
I don't know if that's good or bad,

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.041
but I'm just gonna leave that one there.

00:03:04.041 --> 00:03:04.874
- Hey.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.414
I teach a writing for
art and the artist course

00:03:11.414 --> 00:03:13.560
at Westfield State University,

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:18.030
and I start off the course by
showing my students a paper

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.300
that was heavily destroyed
by one of my professors

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.560
when I was in college.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.560
And I put it up and it said
2001, and you heard the gasps.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.340
- Oh no. (chuckling)

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.543
- 2001? Now stop it.

00:03:32.644 --> 00:03:35.374
Yes. I am that old. Yes.

00:03:35.374 --> 00:03:36.420
- Yeah, yeah.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:39.859
We got that gray going.
Danny, somehow you're keeping,

00:03:39.859 --> 00:03:40.692
you're avoiding that so far.

00:03:40.692 --> 00:03:41.550
So I'm impressed.

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.130
- Yeah, I graduated undergrad in '08

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.630
so I got a few years.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:47.303
- [Nii] All right. You got some time.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:51.930
- I'm hoping I follow my older
brother's aging patterns,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:55.230
so yeah, he looks 35 still.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.060
- Oh my goodness. Well,
if you learn some secrets,

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:57.893
pass them along.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.680
So just to jump in, as my listeners know,

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.060
I often just like to do a check-in.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.140
I appreciate you all kind of sharing

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.070
some of those stories already,

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.838
but just to see how you all
are doing at this point in 2023

00:04:07.838 --> 00:04:10.170
with everything that
you're doing on campus,

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.450
everything we're navigating
as a society. So Danny,

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.300
if we can actually start
with you, that'd be great.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.960
- Yeah, I'm doing really well.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.200
Professionally, I have a few
things that I'm working on

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.729
and hope to finish up a couple of them

00:04:25.729 --> 00:04:29.580
during my sabbatical in 2024.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.080
So I'm really looking forward to that.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.810
I've got something coming
out on political rhetoric.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.760
I've got something coming out on race

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.760
and interpersonal interactions and maybe

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.083
a group processes thing coming out too.

00:04:44.071 --> 00:04:45.300
I'm really excited about that.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.393
I've got some student
collaboration going on as well.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.190
Also putting together my
tenure portfolio here.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:56.283
So it's quite the hectic time.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.870
But yeah, I'm looking forward to getting

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:02.550
all those projects off the desk

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:04.863
during the sabbatical and out.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.613
So everything there is
good. My family is great.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.780
Like, you know, soccer's
a big part of my life

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.493
'cause of my son.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.783
He's awesome. Shout out to
Friends United, by the way.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:25.143
And yeah. So everything
there is just really good,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.860
just chugging along,
trying to like I said,

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:29.910
get this work out.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:30.870
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:31.980
That's great to hear.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.650
Definitely appreciate you
sharing that and not surprising,

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:35.970
you've got your hands in so many things

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:37.020
with so many projects going on,

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:38.130
just from what I know of you,

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:39.644
so that's encouraging to hear.

00:05:39.644 --> 00:05:41.303
- Yep. Yep. Hard for me to say no.

00:05:43.572 --> 00:05:45.240
Thanks for asking though.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.430
- Of course. Imo what about you?

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:50.100
- Yeah, we're plagued by the same demons

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.430
in terms of an inability to say no.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:58.430
I thought I had shaved down
what I was gonna be doing

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.600
for, you know, about a year ago.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:03.420
I canceled a bunch of things.
People were very upset,

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.040
and I am still overwhelmed.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:08.070
There's so much going on right now.

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:11.160
I'm already planning out into 2024,

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:15.090
so this is gonna be interesting. I just,

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:18.960
on the heels of the opening of the first

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.960
major comprehensive solo
exhibition of my studio projects,

00:06:24.397 --> 00:06:26.157
"Benediction" and "in his name,"

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:30.510
that exhibition is titled
"The Hope of Radiance"

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:35.010
at the August Wilson African
American Cultural Center

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:36.740
in Pittsburgh. It's just...

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.491
It is a stellar exhibition.

00:06:41.491 --> 00:06:43.977
The installation is exquisite.

00:06:43.977 --> 00:06:47.565
It's way beyond what I expected it to be.

00:06:47.565 --> 00:06:49.530
And so that's very exciting.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.480
I just curated a group show that is up

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:54.037
at Westfield State University titled

00:06:54.037 --> 00:06:59.037
"Something Extraordinary: A Visual Review

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:00.900
of 'Story' as Timeless."

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.110
So they're eight African diaspora artists

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:06.120
that I've brought together
to have a conversation

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:08.760
about story with their artwork.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:12.840
And it's a very expressive,
amazing exhibition.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.150
The artist's are all very
excited to be a part of it.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:18.180
And I'm currently putting
together a group show

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.190
that will involve myself,

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:22.860
and I think it's six other people now,

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:27.180
but it's called "The Miracle Machine."

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.340
It's gonna be in the fall.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.650
And we don't know what it is we're doing.

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:34.950
There's a musician, there's
three, four artists.

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:36.397
Someone said the title
should be, you know,

00:07:36.397 --> 00:07:39.180
"Four artists, a musician
and a poet walk into a Gap."

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:41.580
And we don't know what we're gonna create.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:44.790
And so UMass Amherst is very excited.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:48.030
It's gonna be like an
incubator think tank space

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:51.753
that will grow over the
course of the fall semester.

00:07:52.834 --> 00:07:55.440
So anxiety, because I don't know exactly

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:58.080
what will come of it, but
that's where we are right now.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.960
So, other things, but I'll stop there.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:01.830
- Yeah, well, I appreciate you

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:03.150
sharing that honestly, as well.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:05.025
I mean, even as we're, for
those who are listening,

00:08:05.025 --> 00:08:06.630
you know, talking on screen,

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.940
I'm thinking about the overlap between,

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:10.590
obviously we do work in different sectors,

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:13.470
but three black men on
this screen in academia,

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:15.420
talking about taking on
lots of different things,

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:16.380
trying to make impact.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:18.660
So there's lots of different
directions that we could go.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:19.950
But I definitely appreciate how you all

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:21.450
have been honest about that.

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.730
And, you know, tenure package coming up,

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.220
all these art exhibitions
coming up, playing in 2024.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.680
I mean, there's so many
aspects that I can relate to

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:29.513
on that as well,

00:08:29.513 --> 00:08:31.710
just in terms of things that
we've been doing and how far

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:33.810
in advance we have to plan,
things we have to say no to

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.040
in order to say yes elsewhere.

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:38.797
And so I think even that
sub-conversation is constructive

00:08:38.797 --> 00:08:41.490
for listeners as we just
think about how we navigate

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.200
in these different spaces.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.033
But even before we get to that,

00:08:44.033 --> 00:08:46.380
'cause now you've both talked
about your work a little bit.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:48.570
I was just gonna have you all
talk about your journey into

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:52.396
your academic professions
too, especially as black men.

00:08:52.396 --> 00:08:54.420
And then as we also think
about this topic of black body.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:56.010
So Imo, why don't we start with you.

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:58.860
How'd you actually get into
this work in the first place?

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:05.280
If there's a short version. (chuckles)

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.200
- I've always been an artist.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:09.630
I was very fortunate.

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:13.590
I'm product of a divorced family.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:16.650
My divorced home, that's
a part of my story.

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:21.270
My two Nigerian parents,
that's a part of my story too.

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:24.410
And, and so there's the...

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:28.410
When I came to New York
to live with my mom

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:32.250
and I began to really,
really jump into the art,

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:36.030
my art, the art scene,
I realized that I wanted

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:40.680
to go into this as an area.

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.060
And she encouraged me.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:44.360
So I went to The High
School of Art and Design

00:09:45.379 --> 00:09:46.500
and by the time I graduated,

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:50.070
I knew I wanted to go
into an academic space

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:53.010
where art wasn't what I was
focusing on necessarily.

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:55.680
So I went to Columbia and I
was an art historian there.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:59.800
And the field of art history
allowed me the chance

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:03.990
to dive into research,
which is what I really,

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:06.000
really wanted to do on some level.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:09.120
But I never stopped making
art. And so it was always very,

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:12.960
very confusing to the institutions
that I've been a part of,

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:15.210
included the one I'm at now
where I'm an art historian.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:16.860
I don't teach studio art courses.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:22.890
How I navigate between
the spaces of art making

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:25.860
and art interpretation.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:30.040
And I think that there has
been this way that people

00:10:31.056 --> 00:10:33.510
have wanted to place me into a box,

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:34.747
especially as a black man.

00:10:34.747 --> 00:10:37.470
"You can't be a creator
and an interpreter.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:38.573
You can't be both." And
I'm like, yes, I can.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:44.730
White folks do this all the time.

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:48.000
They can be curators, they
curate their own spaces,

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:52.140
but there's something
about the way I present.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:55.770
And I think it has a lot to
do with my big black maleness

00:10:55.770 --> 00:11:00.770
that causes people to want to
put me in a particular box.

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:02.070
They didn't like it at Columbia.

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:04.467
They definitely didn't like it at Yale.

00:11:04.467 --> 00:11:07.320
And at Westfield I think
they've embraced it

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:12.320
because they've seen what can
come from it in real time.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:13.680
They've seen it.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:16.590
And so this is a space that
I'm still trying to figure out

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:18.390
I'm being a very bad art historian.

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:20.370
I'm not doing a lot of writing right now,

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:21.810
but the work that I'm doing

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:23.850
is certainly contributing to the field.

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:28.803
And so this is where I am when
I speak to other students,

00:11:28.803 --> 00:11:31.440
when I speak to students
and other people who

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:33.480
are thinking about diving
into art, they're like,

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:35.850
well, how did you do
this? And how is it like?

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:39.150
Well this is the pathway
that allowed me to,

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:41.524
this is the pathway I
chose to be a researcher

00:11:41.524 --> 00:11:44.790
and to do art, not art on the side,

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:48.150
but art as really the center,

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:52.200
the creation process as
the center of my research.

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:54.180
And it's informed everything.

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:55.013
- Wow. Yeah.

00:11:55.013 --> 00:11:56.580
I appreciate you sharing
that honestly too. I mean,

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:58.920
it's just from, you know,
kind of childhood on,

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:01.770
there's a lot we'll definitely
come back to and unpack there

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:02.730
that you've pulled out.

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:03.570
But just before we do that,

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:05.730
just wanna jump to Daniel
real quick and just hear

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:07.971
your story too about, you know,
how you've gotten into this,

00:12:07.971 --> 00:12:11.850
this academic endeavor across
so many different aspects

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:12.723
of sociology.

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:16.560
- A lot of my own personal interests,

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:19.680
a lot of timing and a little bit of luck.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:20.513
So I'll go back.

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:25.710
My parents had different religions.

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:28.140
Neither one of them was
particularly convicted.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:30.690
Neither one of them ever took me to

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:32.583
either place of worship ever.

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:36.903
So I grew up without a conception of God.

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:40.680
Time when, that was, being an atheist,

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:43.740
let alone like, never
having believed in God,

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:46.290
was seen as very strange.

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:49.260
So that was always something that I liked

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:51.750
reading about and stuff like that.

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:53.313
So I had that one interest.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:57.120
At the same time, my parents were also,

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.373
my mom's Jewish, my dad's black.

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:04.170
And so there was always really
interesting racial dynamics.

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:05.880
I grew up in an all black neighborhood.

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:08.700
My mom was the only white
person in the neighborhood.

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:11.160
So I always had lots of interesting

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.077
things happening about race.

00:13:13.077 --> 00:13:16.920
And then my mom was born in the forties,

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:21.180
so she was raised at a time
when being Jewish wasn't all

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:23.163
the way encompassed into whiteness yet.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.220
Later she accepted that she was white,

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:29.310
but up until like the seventies
she told me she fought it.

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:33.303
And, you know, so lots of
interesting things there.

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:37.710
And so I just always wanted to, you know,

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:40.713
explore the intersections
of a lot of those things.

00:13:41.978 --> 00:13:44.730
And you know, I was one
of the kids that would

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:46.880
skip school sometimes to go to the library

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:51.020
'cause I was really focused on
learning about these things.

00:13:51.020 --> 00:13:53.328
So I was reading philosophy
books and all that

00:13:53.328 --> 00:13:55.920
at a really early age.

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:57.900
Had aspirations to be a philosopher,

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.030
actually got to Rutgers.

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:02.460
I went there 'cause at the
time it was the number one

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:04.710
philosophy program in the world.

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:08.610
Or maybe tied with Oxford and took

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:10.710
16 or 17 philosophy classes.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:14.130
Loved my professors and
was the only black kid

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:16.173
in every single class except for-

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:18.720
And as I started reading this stuff,

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:21.420
I was like, man, this is
really stodgy. I don't...

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:23.570
There were a bunch of
issues I had with it.

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:28.680
So right at the end of
my undergrad career,

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:31.140
I had a bunch of professors
come to me and this, you know,

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:33.120
this was lucky for me, they said,

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:35.580
we think you have a lot of potential.

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:39.633
Have you ever considered these
other tangential sort of,

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:43.830
careers or where you want to go?

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:45.903
And they suggested public policy.

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:49.620
I actually had one put me
in touch with Cornell West

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:51.630
who talked to me and
we developed something

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:54.000
that I could have possibly done.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:57.510
Personal circumstances
led me back to Maryland

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:00.663
and their sociology
program really embraced me.

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:06.877
And something like Imo
said, about 6'1", like 215,

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:10.740
and I literally had some of the
black professors at Maryland

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:12.750
and this was a really
important part of my life,

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:16.440
come to me, "You need to know
when you walk around here

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:19.650
the way you do and you
dress the way you do that

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:21.550
people are gonna see it a certain way.

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:23.790
It doesn't have to be good or bad,

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:26.910
but you need to be conscious
of it so you can operate

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:29.876
within these bounds and get
to where you need to go."

00:15:29.876 --> 00:15:34.110
Dr. Patricia Hill Collins
was one of those people,

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:36.870
Dr. Kris Marsh, who just wrote a book

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:39.000
on the black middle class
called "Love Jones,"

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:42.420
was one of those people, Dr. Rashawn Ray.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:45.813
So just having people fall into
my life at the right times.

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:51.390
And then as soon as I got in
the classroom, I loved it.

00:15:51.390 --> 00:15:55.170
I could do and say so
many things that I thought

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:57.990
were valuable to me and the class.

00:15:57.990 --> 00:16:02.100
And just since I TA'd my
first class, I was like,

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:05.700
I think this is such
a worthwhile existence

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:09.030
to be a professor, to engage in work

00:16:09.030 --> 00:16:11.070
that'll eventually
trickle in the classrooms

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:14.883
and influence what people
read, what people think.

00:16:16.415 --> 00:16:19.290
And so yeah, it was
sort of just a path that

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:23.443
just sort of happened before
me and I just took it.

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:26.340
- Yeah, I definitely appreciate
you sharing that story.

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:27.860
I mean, I think a lot of
people just, you know,

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:30.360
in hearing that will just
be able to gain insight

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:31.650
and maybe even understanding that

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:33.000
they didn't have before. Just, you know,

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:35.070
'cause sometimes I know for students,

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:36.480
when they walk into our classrooms

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:37.800
or see us in different settings,

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:39.964
they don't know the journey to get there.

00:16:39.964 --> 00:16:41.262
It just helps.

00:16:41.262 --> 00:16:43.740
- They're all so much
better high school students

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:47.280
than I was, or almost
all of them at least.

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:50.110
- Yeah. Which is scary for me sometimes.

00:16:51.937 --> 00:16:54.600
So again, just appreciate
you both sharing that.

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:56.824
I mean, I know we're
gonna talk a little bit

00:16:56.824 --> 00:16:59.220
about black biased representation
in art and society,

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:00.810
but just with what you both mentioned,

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:02.190
I just wanted to lean
in a little bit to like,

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:04.410
your own experience as black men.

00:17:04.410 --> 00:17:06.810
Like what are some of the things,

00:17:06.810 --> 00:17:07.860
'cause I know you both talked about

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:09.300
people kind of prepping you for that

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:10.470
in some ways or things that you saw.

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:13.110
What are things that surprised
you in your experience

00:17:13.110 --> 00:17:18.110
and kind of how have you
navigated to thrive in a sense?

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:20.603
Dan, we'll reverse it and
start with you this time.

00:17:22.830 --> 00:17:27.830
- So again, some of the things
that surprised me were just,

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:34.020
what was normal to me was
not, you know, acceptable,

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:37.860
was not within the bounds of
what people were looking for.

00:17:37.860 --> 00:17:40.770
And definitely not what
people expected. Like I said,

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:45.770
I almost always went to
schools that were 75-80% black

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:50.040
even when I was in like
really advanced programs.

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:53.130
And so being smart, being articulate,

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:55.443
those things were not surprising.

00:17:56.490 --> 00:17:59.070
And then all of a sudden I get to college

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:02.130
and people are offended
that I'm raising my hand

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:05.670
so much and that I have
opinions and that my God,

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:07.830
he actually knows the answer
before everybody else.

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:11.043
And I was not ready for that.

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:16.653
And so I definitely needed
people to help me out.

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:20.370
Again, understanding
that, navigating that,

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:24.780
not letting that bother me and, you know,

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:28.410
because those are really
good and important qualities

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:31.200
for the professor being in academia

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:33.000
and not to let those qualities, you know,

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:35.493
sort of be drained out or beaten outta me.

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:40.140
So again, now that I know
what I know as a sociologist,

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:42.030
I shouldn't have been
surprised by those things,

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:44.972
but I was, you know, 17, 18 years old.

00:18:44.972 --> 00:18:48.060
And I had only had the
experiences that I had growing up

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:49.440
in Prince George's County.

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:51.960
Like I just showed my classes now,

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:54.990
we're talking about
contemporary segregation.

00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:57.633
I live where my kids go
to school, PG County,

00:18:58.620 --> 00:19:03.620
the school system has
120,000 kids, only 3% white.

00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:08.640
Right? And so that is
the kind of experience

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:10.350
that I had basically.

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:13.443
And then again, going
to these PWI big states,

00:19:14.585 --> 00:19:17.130
it was something that I needed help with.

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:19.020
And like I said, I've just been lucky

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:21.630
to have people there for me.

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:23.580
- Yeah. Just to follow
up a little bit on that,

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:24.417
and you talked about this a little bit,

00:19:24.417 --> 00:19:28.200
but how are you helping
or informing or guiding

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:29.640
your students in that process too?

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:31.140
Even as you think about
your own experience,

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:32.640
the knowledge you have as a sociologist

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.220
and paying it forward, so to speak?

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:36.303
- Multiple things.

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:40.980
So one like, and sometimes they ask me,

00:19:40.980 --> 00:19:43.080
but I generally dress like this every day.

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:46.917
Like in, not awful,
but like in a way where

00:19:46.917 --> 00:19:49.683
I'm like, living my authentic black body.

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:52.710
To show them that, you know,

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:54.780
maybe some of these
stereotypes aren't right.

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:58.320
And, you know, I think sort
of just living that every day.

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:02.400
I know like Jody Armour and
Cornell West wear their afros

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:04.320
for the same sort of reason.

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:07.743
Like Jody Armour says
it's his hoodie. Right?

00:20:09.030 --> 00:20:13.470
And so I try to live it
and I also, you know,

00:20:13.470 --> 00:20:16.623
through my experiences,
try to, and I have,

00:20:16.623 --> 00:20:19.230
I've been all over different demographics

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:20.943
because of my parents,

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:27.360
that I can sort of translate
a lot of what I have learned

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:30.450
across different spaces.

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:34.200
And so that's really what I
try to do in the classroom

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:37.230
is bridge any gaps, use myself as a foil

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:40.470
so we can just like, have a medium

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:42.753
to talk about these issues.

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:48.660
And yeah, so like I
said, I really try to use

00:20:48.660 --> 00:20:53.430
that strength in terms of
across different groups.

00:20:53.430 --> 00:20:56.460
- Yeah. That's really
good. Definitely powerful.

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:57.690
Imo anything you wanna add,

00:20:57.690 --> 00:20:59.430
either to answer the question
about your own navigation

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:01.410
or even riff off of
anything that Danny said?

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:06.410
- Yeah, I was gonna mention
the notion of serendipity

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:10.170
or luck or like there's...

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:15.170
You know, for all of the
intelligence that people

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:18.300
have claimed I have or that

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:20.523
I have thought that I may have had.

00:21:21.540 --> 00:21:26.430
I think so, so much of
my arrival, where I'm at,

00:21:26.430 --> 00:21:31.430
has come from just the
kindness of just good people.

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:36.920
People who decided to
care at a particular time.

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:42.573
The idea that I've arrived
where I'm at by myself,

00:21:45.482 --> 00:21:46.980
it's a false one.

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:49.320
And so I always find it really interesting

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:51.570
when I encounter, go to a conference,

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:55.087
or you speak to people
and it's almost like

00:21:55.087 --> 00:22:00.030
they've prepared to be
Dr. So-and-so for so long

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:03.720
that you get a sense that-

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:05.310
The phone going off, for example,

00:22:05.310 --> 00:22:07.410
in the middle of a conversation like this

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:10.800
would be catastrophic, right?

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:13.925
And I've just never been
able to arrive at this place

00:22:13.925 --> 00:22:16.990
where I take myself so seriously

00:22:18.150 --> 00:22:23.116
that I lose my sense
of humanity. You know?

00:22:23.116 --> 00:22:26.340
And I think that's what my
students connect with the most.

00:22:26.340 --> 00:22:28.260
I will sometimes dress like this in class

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:30.362
and sometimes I'm wearing a t-shirt

00:22:30.362 --> 00:22:31.650
'cause I'm coming to class from the studio

00:22:31.650 --> 00:22:33.390
and I'm going back to the studio to work

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:34.590
and I tell them what I'm working on.

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:35.910
I'm like, I'm working on this drawing

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:37.650
and it's kicking my butt right now.

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:40.150
You know, that's why there's
charcoal on my shirt.

00:22:41.100 --> 00:22:46.100
But when I first came in
as a professor in 2009,

00:22:47.610 --> 00:22:50.850
I thought I was supposed to be
wearing blazers or a suit or,

00:22:50.850 --> 00:22:52.953
I just had this feeling that,

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:57.720
because I teach at a
predominantly white institution

00:22:57.720 --> 00:23:00.750
and there was a sense that it was my,

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:05.336
part of my job or burden
was to make it clear

00:23:05.336 --> 00:23:08.070
that I'm worthy of this.

00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:11.210
That as a black person I can do this.

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:15.420
And to demonstrate to the
white kids in my class

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:20.220
that I really understand
my field and to demonstrate

00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:24.352
to my department that I
was going to do this well.

00:23:24.352 --> 00:23:27.489
Now...

00:23:27.489 --> 00:23:31.590
Everybody could see
and continue to see now

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:36.030
that the level and the quality of work

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:39.000
I was bringing was way beyond

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:41.460
what they were even asking for.

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:43.960
But it was so hard for me to accept it

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:49.440
because there was this feeling
that I'm just above water.

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:52.863
I'm not actually there.
And at any given time,

00:23:55.019 --> 00:23:57.300
a rouge wave could come
over and just drown me.

00:23:57.300 --> 00:23:59.710
So when I first got
into Columbia University

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:03.150
from the High School of Art and Design,

00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:06.000
it was a really, really big
deal for my high school.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:09.300
I was wait-listed Harvard,
that was a huge deal, you know?

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:14.300
And I just knew that they
had made a mistake. In fact,

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:21.000
the first two to four,

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:24.420
I'll say two months of my
time at Columbia University

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:28.630
in my dorm room, I waited for notification

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:33.810
from the head of whoever
to say, we made a mistake.

00:24:33.810 --> 00:24:35.550
You're not supposed to be here.

00:24:35.550 --> 00:24:40.500
Because I was struggling
when I first got there.

00:24:40.500 --> 00:24:43.110
I was stumbling through these classes.

00:24:43.110 --> 00:24:45.390
I was trying to figure
out who I was and see

00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:47.910
all this sea of white folks
and the few black people

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:50.910
that were at school were
trying to figure out

00:24:50.910 --> 00:24:53.640
who they were too. And there's insecurity

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:55.890
and there are weird things
that are said because of that.

00:24:55.890 --> 00:24:58.020
And it's like, I can look
back on it now and be like,

00:24:58.020 --> 00:25:00.120
oh God, we were all so young and stupid

00:25:00.120 --> 00:25:01.320
and just trying to figure things out.

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:05.160
But it still haunts me,
some of what I went through

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:09.990
that first semester until I
figured out how to do undergrad.

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:12.990
And then when I got to
Yale, it was even worse.

00:25:12.990 --> 00:25:16.230
It was even worse because
this thing that we study

00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:20.160
called art, art history,
this thing that we study,

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:23.040
which for me is not just a
thing to study. I live it.

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:24.720
I roll around in charcoal and paint.

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:26.523
This is what I do for fun, right?

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:32.670
It had become so dry and
philosophical. And here I am,

00:25:32.670 --> 00:25:35.280
big black me in the class and
they're asking me a question

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:37.620
about the thing we just
read. And I'm like,

00:25:37.620 --> 00:25:39.603
did we all read the same thing?

00:25:41.042 --> 00:25:44.583
Because my take was so
violently different.

00:25:45.630 --> 00:25:49.920
And the way I spoke was
so casual and I'm like,

00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:51.900
this is art, I do this
every day. Like, no,

00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:54.090
I don't care if you're using big words.

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:57.113
Like I do this every day, I know this.

00:25:58.294 --> 00:26:02.055
And I just, it took me such
a long time to understand

00:26:02.055 --> 00:26:07.020
that I was just coming from a
completely different worldview

00:26:07.020 --> 00:26:09.660
from most of the people
at these institutions.

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:14.130
And that it wasn't my job
to change my worldview.

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:17.250
It was my job to just shine in the way

00:26:17.250 --> 00:26:20.926
that I could in myself.
But it took a while.

00:26:20.926 --> 00:26:22.530
It took a while. I wish I could say that

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:24.990
it was with great confidence
that I walked through

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:26.700
the halls of these institutions.

00:26:26.700 --> 00:26:31.077
But even now, I shudder as I remember,

00:26:31.077 --> 00:26:33.060
I shudder as I remember. Yeah.

00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:34.410
- Wow. Well that's real.

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:35.730
And I know that people listening,

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:37.080
there's gonna be lots
of folks that are gonna

00:26:37.080 --> 00:26:39.750
be able to relate to that
too. And then also in a sense,

00:26:39.750 --> 00:26:42.180
even get encouragement from
you sharing the challenges

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:44.160
to know that okay, if they're
navigating through that,

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:45.690
it's not just them. Because like, I mean,

00:26:45.690 --> 00:26:48.630
even as you described that
there's so much isolation in how

00:26:48.630 --> 00:26:50.313
you just described that even
though you saw other people

00:26:50.313 --> 00:26:53.220
navigating those same
pieces in isolated pods.

00:26:53.220 --> 00:26:55.200
And I know my mental health
piece is coming out too.

00:26:55.200 --> 00:26:57.600
But that's the first
thing that came to mind

00:26:57.600 --> 00:26:59.250
as you were talking. Definitely.

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:01.860
- I don't think people talk
about the failures enough.

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:04.170
And I don't think people,
and when I say people,

00:27:04.170 --> 00:27:05.490
I mean in general, academics.

00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:08.820
But I think that black
academics in particular

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:12.090
should grow more comfortable
discussing the failures

00:27:12.090 --> 00:27:16.230
because these spaces
haven't gotten any easier

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:19.350
for the next generation
of black and brown people.

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:21.180
They haven't become easier.

00:27:21.180 --> 00:27:24.564
We're dealing with the same things, right?

00:27:24.564 --> 00:27:28.050
And so the idea of not sharing, my gosh,

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:30.720
one of the things I share with
my students is I tell them,

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:33.660
if you, you know, if you
do well this semester,

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:35.010
remind me at close to end of the semester,

00:27:35.010 --> 00:27:37.410
I will tell you my how I almost got kicked

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:38.910
out of Yale story.

00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:41.880
You know, about how the paper that I,

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:46.880
the papers that I submitted
were so not up to par

00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:50.910
because of all of my anxiety
that they almost threw me out.

00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:53.460
I shared this conversation
openly with anyone

00:27:53.460 --> 00:27:57.029
who will listen that I
graduated, got my PhD

00:27:57.029 --> 00:27:59.059
and I'm doing very well.

00:27:59.059 --> 00:28:01.230
But they almost threw me out.

00:28:01.230 --> 00:28:05.340
They almost threw me out because
I was looking for myself.

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:08.670
And it took me so long to figure out

00:28:08.670 --> 00:28:10.323
who I was at that school.

00:28:11.550 --> 00:28:12.870
But I'm so comfortable sharing that.

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:14.970
I've seen other scholars
cringe when I talk about it.

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:16.350
I'm like, why are you cringing?

00:28:16.350 --> 00:28:17.313
It's my story.

00:28:18.170 --> 00:28:19.410
And somebody needs this.

00:28:19.410 --> 00:28:21.780
- Exactly. Yeah.

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:23.010
Danny, anything you wanna comment on?

00:28:23.010 --> 00:28:26.640
Either like from your own
experience or your lens?

00:28:26.640 --> 00:28:27.720
'Cause you talked about that too, like,

00:28:27.720 --> 00:28:29.550
now what you know, as a sociologist,

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:31.200
how that also impacted you looking back

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:32.033
and thinking about, okay,

00:28:32.033 --> 00:28:35.100
that's why I was struggling
here or navigating this way.

00:28:35.100 --> 00:28:37.200
Any thoughts in general?

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:38.033
- For sure.

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:41.670
And again, one, it was just,

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:43.800
my background was very different.

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:48.800
Like, both of my brothers
were street dudes,

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:50.550
had been in jail a lot.

00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:55.550
And like, those are still
part of my influences.

00:28:57.047 --> 00:29:01.593
And yeah, it just, I have
something specific actually.

00:29:02.520 --> 00:29:05.280
So, and again, it has to do

00:29:05.280 --> 00:29:09.270
with mannerisms and what's
normalized and what's taught.

00:29:09.270 --> 00:29:14.253
So again, grew up talking,

00:29:15.540 --> 00:29:19.860
conversing a certain way and
for whatever reason, again,

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:23.670
maybe it's because of
where I went to school,

00:29:23.670 --> 00:29:27.480
never had grammar in any level of school.

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:30.450
Not in third grade, not in
fifth grade, not in ninth grade,

00:29:30.450 --> 00:29:33.660
not in 12th grade. Never. Never. Right?

00:29:33.660 --> 00:29:36.720
So I'm in all these AP
language classes in high school

00:29:36.720 --> 00:29:40.020
and like, I write well, so I'm getting B's

00:29:40.020 --> 00:29:42.993
but I'm always getting my papers
all marked up and whatnot.

00:29:44.700 --> 00:29:49.330
And then I get to college
and the compository writing

00:29:50.340 --> 00:29:51.900
or expository writing,

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:53.900
I forget what the name of the class was.

00:29:55.830 --> 00:29:57.570
I'm getting really good grades in that.

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:00.960
And my teacher is really
impressed with the way

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:03.417
that I can interpret
passages and all of that.

00:30:03.417 --> 00:30:06.870
And she even assigns me
to help a couple students

00:30:06.870 --> 00:30:08.970
in the class that are struggling.

00:30:08.970 --> 00:30:11.850
And then we take a final in that class,

00:30:11.850 --> 00:30:14.850
which isn't graded by the
professor, but graded by

00:30:14.850 --> 00:30:18.030
a random grad student in
the English department.

00:30:18.030 --> 00:30:21.450
And it's graded very technically.

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:26.160
And I failed that final,
first semester freshman year.

00:30:26.160 --> 00:30:28.297
And I had all A's in other classes.

00:30:28.297 --> 00:30:30.840
So I was like, I can do this.

00:30:30.840 --> 00:30:35.840
But it was this thing that I
was never taught and combined

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:41.880
with the fact that how I
normally talk and normally write

00:30:41.880 --> 00:30:46.410
is culturally alien to
a lot of those people

00:30:46.410 --> 00:30:49.423
that I ended up failing
that class and that,

00:30:49.423 --> 00:30:53.580
you know, again, my mom was
really supportive of me in that,

00:30:53.580 --> 00:30:55.800
my dad was really supportive of me.

00:30:55.800 --> 00:30:58.300
My other professors were
really supportive of me

00:30:59.820 --> 00:31:01.593
to not let that get me down.

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:06.087
But my second semester I had
to repeat a class. Right?

00:31:06.087 --> 00:31:08.850
And I still ended up
graduating with honors

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:11.043
in philosophy and sociology.

00:31:12.210 --> 00:31:15.510
But those trials are
real and a lot of times

00:31:15.510 --> 00:31:16.890
just aren't prepared for them.

00:31:16.890 --> 00:31:20.250
And you can't know, unless
you have somebody who has

00:31:20.250 --> 00:31:23.190
the experience and knowledge,
you can't really know

00:31:23.190 --> 00:31:26.970
that you're not prepared
for certain spaces.

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:27.840
And so.

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:29.390
- No, you can't know!

00:31:30.443 --> 00:31:33.150
- I mean, I got an A in
the class the second time.

00:31:33.150 --> 00:31:37.230
- Yeah. Wow. I mean that's
really, that's really poignant.

00:31:37.230 --> 00:31:40.680
But I mean, I'm trying to
figure out how to say this,

00:31:40.680 --> 00:31:45.680
but it just speaks to that
whole meritocracy commentary.

00:31:45.690 --> 00:31:47.490
I know a lot of people have
been having that conversation.

00:31:47.490 --> 00:31:49.200
Some might hear us and say,
oh, we're hearing this again.

00:31:49.200 --> 00:31:54.200
But I still think sometimes
people just don't want to admit

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:56.190
that's 'cause you know,
you had another TA,

00:31:56.190 --> 00:31:58.080
everything shifted at last minute

00:31:58.080 --> 00:31:59.880
and someone from the
outside could be like, oh,

00:31:59.880 --> 00:32:03.240
well he just didn't earn the same

00:32:03.240 --> 00:32:04.590
X, Y, and Z as everybody else.

00:32:04.590 --> 00:32:08.100
Ignoring the fact of what
you were exposed to early on,

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:09.660
how that impacts you in that situation

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:11.640
and how the other professor
was giving you feedback.

00:32:11.640 --> 00:32:13.080
Like all of that.

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:15.000
And then how that's just
used to kind of, you know,

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.700
keep people in their place,
for lack of a better word.

00:32:18.600 --> 00:32:20.800
- Still can barely use
a comma or semicolon.

00:32:21.810 --> 00:32:24.903
Drop 'em in there haphazardly
and let editors fix it now.

00:32:28.500 --> 00:32:30.150
- Yeah. Somebody needs to hear that.

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:33.320
Somebody's gonna need to hear
what you just said because...

00:32:35.250 --> 00:32:36.090
Yeah.

00:32:36.090 --> 00:32:37.500
It's such a-

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:41.220
I wish that I could look back
on all of these experiences

00:32:41.220 --> 00:32:42.450
with fondness,

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:46.967
but there was so much of it
that was uphill fighting for me.

00:32:48.570 --> 00:32:52.323
And so like, I-

00:32:53.190 --> 00:32:55.410
But I do look at my
undergraduate experiences

00:32:55.410 --> 00:32:57.390
with fondness because like I said,

00:32:57.390 --> 00:33:00.510
there were people who
chose to just be kind.

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:03.003
They saw a diamond in the rough.

00:33:04.410 --> 00:33:07.350
But I bring this up because
it was those decisions

00:33:07.350 --> 00:33:11.610
that certain people made,
you know, Adam Rothman,

00:33:11.610 --> 00:33:16.610
who was at that time, a PhD
student teaching a course,

00:33:17.100 --> 00:33:21.540
and he's doing big things now,
but like, he pulled me aside.

00:33:21.540 --> 00:33:24.600
He's like, Imo, when I see
an A I'll give you one.

00:33:24.600 --> 00:33:26.100
Here's what you need to do.

00:33:26.100 --> 00:33:30.210
And a lot of my issue was scaling back

00:33:30.210 --> 00:33:31.560
how much I was saying,

00:33:31.560 --> 00:33:35.790
because I was able to get
to the crux of my arguments

00:33:35.790 --> 00:33:38.070
much faster with shorter papers.

00:33:38.070 --> 00:33:39.960
There were two different
professors that pulled me aside

00:33:39.960 --> 00:33:42.210
and said, oh God, you're
doing this all wrong.

00:33:42.210 --> 00:33:46.230
You wrote seven pages here.
But the last four pages

00:33:46.230 --> 00:33:47.640
were brilliant.

00:33:47.640 --> 00:33:49.731
The first two made no sense.

00:33:49.731 --> 00:33:54.731
Someone had to teach me how
to harness what they all said

00:33:57.300 --> 00:33:58.950
was a gift for writing.

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:00.840
They all said, no, no, no,

00:34:00.840 --> 00:34:03.960
you are one of the best
writer we've ever seen

00:34:03.960 --> 00:34:07.020
at this stage. I'm like, I am? Like, yes.

00:34:07.020 --> 00:34:08.429
Then why don't I have an A?

00:34:08.429 --> 00:34:10.443
Because this is a terrible paper,

00:34:11.430 --> 00:34:13.350
but we can see what you can do.

00:34:13.350 --> 00:34:18.240
And so pulling me aside and
showing me line by line.

00:34:18.240 --> 00:34:20.910
But it took time. It took time.

00:34:20.910 --> 00:34:24.480
And so now as a professor,

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:28.230
I remember that, I remind
myself of that and my students,

00:34:28.230 --> 00:34:32.250
I show them my old papers
and I sit with the ones

00:34:32.250 --> 00:34:33.930
who really want to know.

00:34:33.930 --> 00:34:38.280
And we go through it line
by line until we understand,

00:34:38.280 --> 00:34:42.390
you know, how we can be better. You know?

00:34:42.390 --> 00:34:44.283
But I can't, this-

00:34:45.570 --> 00:34:47.767
For anybody who would say, you know, oh,

00:34:47.767 --> 00:34:50.010
you know, well that has
nothing to do with race.

00:34:50.010 --> 00:34:51.450
You're making everything about race. Like,

00:34:51.450 --> 00:34:52.590
why are you talking about race?

00:34:52.590 --> 00:34:55.920
Why would you bring up race
in this kind of instance,

00:34:55.920 --> 00:34:58.383
because race was such a big part of it,

00:34:59.220 --> 00:35:04.220
the fear of speaking in those
white spaces because you felt

00:35:05.070 --> 00:35:09.060
like anything you said would
represent your entire race.

00:35:09.060 --> 00:35:11.700
You know, having certain
professors pull me aside and say,

00:35:11.700 --> 00:35:15.750
Imo you've been really
quiet in class lately. Why?

00:35:15.750 --> 00:35:17.790
And not being able to admit that I heard

00:35:17.790 --> 00:35:20.100
two white girls snickering in the corner

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:22.980
when I was speaking
and immediately thought

00:35:22.980 --> 00:35:24.630
that I sounded stupid.

00:35:24.630 --> 00:35:28.380
The professors had to find
ways to get me to speak.

00:35:28.380 --> 00:35:32.700
And then once I was open,
it was like, it was over.

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:36.490
But, race is so much a part still

00:35:37.844 --> 00:35:39.873
of this college experience.

00:35:40.772 --> 00:35:45.772
Especially for black men in,
in schools such as these.

00:35:46.050 --> 00:35:50.190
- Yeah. Yeah. Really well
said. And I appreciate, again,

00:35:50.190 --> 00:35:52.287
just how open you are
with your students too,

00:35:52.287 --> 00:35:53.910
and just kind of taking
them on that journey.

00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:56.190
Danny, you look like you're
gonna reflect on something.

00:35:56.190 --> 00:35:59.703
- Oh, no, I just really
agreed with what he said.

00:36:01.470 --> 00:36:04.263
Try to employ a bunch of
the similar practices.

00:36:05.160 --> 00:36:07.020
- Can I say one more thing?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:08.190
- There were these moments,

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:09.690
I know we're talking about black bodies,

00:36:09.690 --> 00:36:11.373
or we will or we already are.

00:36:12.430 --> 00:36:14.310
And there were these, there were-

00:36:14.310 --> 00:36:16.830
I don't remember every moment of my time

00:36:16.830 --> 00:36:19.770
at these institutions
but there are a couple

00:36:19.770 --> 00:36:21.540
that stand out to me.

00:36:21.540 --> 00:36:24.273
And there was one when I wanna say Yale,

00:36:26.051 --> 00:36:29.100
in the music library is easily
one of the most extraordinary

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:33.120
beautiful things ever constructed.

00:36:33.120 --> 00:36:35.670
It's just such a
beautiful space, you know?

00:36:35.670 --> 00:36:39.623
So I would just go in there
sometimes and just sit there,

00:36:39.623 --> 00:36:40.953
you know?

00:36:42.559 --> 00:36:46.710
And I remember, and there
were these moments where

00:36:46.710 --> 00:36:50.790
I would completely forget
who I was in this space.

00:36:50.790 --> 00:36:54.060
And it was one particular time
I was walking by in there,

00:36:54.060 --> 00:36:56.280
just, I greeted somebody really warmly

00:36:56.280 --> 00:37:00.650
and they just looked me up
and down and I was like, okay.

00:37:02.040 --> 00:37:06.540
And then I went to the bathroom
and I caught my reflection.

00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:10.380
I'm seeing this big black, always sweaty.

00:37:10.380 --> 00:37:15.380
There's a sweat on my book
bag. And I'm just like,

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:16.223
damn, I forgot.

00:37:17.490 --> 00:37:20.400
I remember that moment.

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:24.213
When I saw myself in
the mirror and I'm like,

00:37:25.320 --> 00:37:27.360
damn, I forgot. Like.

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:31.560
Why would anybody not greet
me or smile? You know?

00:37:31.560 --> 00:37:34.230
And I was just reminded in that space.

00:37:34.230 --> 00:37:36.960
It's like, negro, this ain't your space.

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:39.397
You know, this is how I felt there.

00:37:39.397 --> 00:37:40.800
And I had a few moments like that.

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:43.200
But this is one that
just, I'm reminded of.

00:37:43.200 --> 00:37:45.060
It's not that it was all gloom and doom.

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:47.340
I had some wonderful things
that happened at these

00:37:47.340 --> 00:37:51.063
institutions to there in
undergrad studio Catesh.

00:37:52.508 --> 00:37:56.580
I realized that my way into art history,

00:37:56.580 --> 00:37:59.790
my way into art history as a study- Okay.

00:37:59.790 --> 00:38:02.670
My way into research
of art history happened

00:38:02.670 --> 00:38:05.193
through ethnography,
happened through sociology.

00:38:09.107 --> 00:38:12.300
Opened up my whole
worldview. Like, I'm like,

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:14.190
wait, wait, wait, wait,
wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.

00:38:14.190 --> 00:38:18.210
So I could study African
and African American artists

00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:20.790
as a sociological project?

00:38:20.790 --> 00:38:23.850
And he had me do an
independent study with him.

00:38:23.850 --> 00:38:28.850
It's not easy, but he walked
me through that slowly,

00:38:29.100 --> 00:38:31.200
methodically, carefully,

00:38:31.200 --> 00:38:33.810
and brought me to this
place where I could begin

00:38:33.810 --> 00:38:37.500
to understand how art
history and sociology

00:38:37.500 --> 00:38:40.901
and archeology and all of
these things are very, very,

00:38:40.901 --> 00:38:43.530
they're not as divided as you would think,

00:38:43.530 --> 00:38:45.450
depending on what it is
you're trying to study.

00:38:45.450 --> 00:38:47.520
As an Africanist in art history,

00:38:47.520 --> 00:38:49.680
these things are all very, very connected.

00:38:49.680 --> 00:38:51.330
And so I had good moments.

00:38:51.330 --> 00:38:54.570
I just wanna make it
clear that the race part

00:38:54.570 --> 00:38:59.040
is not something that's easily divorcable.

00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:00.840
- It's part of it. Yeah.

00:39:00.840 --> 00:39:03.670
- Well, I'll add one real
quick thing. And that is

00:39:05.760 --> 00:39:10.760
both of us went to undergrad
at like the peak of like,

00:39:11.490 --> 00:39:13.890
colorblindness in this system.

00:39:13.890 --> 00:39:17.610
We weren't supposed to talk
about those things, right?

00:39:17.610 --> 00:39:18.630
That's when they were like, oh,

00:39:18.630 --> 00:39:21.600
get the chip off your
shoulder or whatever.

00:39:21.600 --> 00:39:24.870
I think there's now a little
more space for talking about

00:39:24.870 --> 00:39:26.703
those sorts of experiences.

00:39:27.540 --> 00:39:32.540
But again, like 15, 20 years
ago there really wasn't.

00:39:34.050 --> 00:39:34.883
So, yeah.

00:39:34.883 --> 00:39:36.513
- Yeah, that's a really good point.

00:39:37.440 --> 00:39:39.270
Just, I mean, just to pick
up on that a little bit too,

00:39:39.270 --> 00:39:40.740
'cause the one thing we were
gonna talk about is just like

00:39:40.740 --> 00:39:44.820
how you all think about these
topics in your current roles,

00:39:44.820 --> 00:39:46.980
both what you do in
the classroom and like,

00:39:46.980 --> 00:39:48.190
how it impacts society. So Daniel,

00:39:48.190 --> 00:39:49.440
I was just gonna follow up with you.

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:50.357
Like, how does that,

00:39:50.357 --> 00:39:52.290
'cause I know we've talked a lot about

00:39:52.290 --> 00:39:53.907
the wealth gap pieces and
some of those other things

00:39:53.907 --> 00:39:56.760
and how you introduce your
students to those concepts.

00:39:56.760 --> 00:39:58.500
So how do you think about, I mean,

00:39:58.500 --> 00:40:00.000
this isn't exactly what you do,

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:02.730
but how you think about the
concept of a black body,

00:40:02.730 --> 00:40:05.220
even as you're teaching all
these different classes on all

00:40:05.220 --> 00:40:07.710
these intersecting topics and
how you kind of pass that on

00:40:07.710 --> 00:40:09.690
to your students to really make sure

00:40:09.690 --> 00:40:11.790
they catch the realities.

00:40:11.790 --> 00:40:12.813
- Right. Good.

00:40:13.800 --> 00:40:16.290
One of the things I try
to drive home across

00:40:16.290 --> 00:40:19.470
all of the different
subjects that we cover is

00:40:19.470 --> 00:40:23.910
that there is so much
evidence that there is

00:40:23.910 --> 00:40:27.813
a social and racial
hierarchy in this country.

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:30.930
Blacks. The most consistent thing

00:40:30.930 --> 00:40:34.410
is that black people are
at the bottom. Right?

00:40:34.410 --> 00:40:36.540
And so I try to,

00:40:36.540 --> 00:40:40.380
depending on what we're covering that week

00:40:40.380 --> 00:40:44.340
or whatever relates
something to that, right?

00:40:44.340 --> 00:40:45.573
And so,

00:40:47.460 --> 00:40:52.350
like today we were talking
about implicit bias, right?

00:40:52.350 --> 00:40:57.350
And to literally show an
experiment done of, you know,

00:40:57.960 --> 00:41:00.600
here's a white kid acting
this way in a park.

00:41:00.600 --> 00:41:03.510
Here's a black kid acting
this way in a park.

00:41:03.510 --> 00:41:07.803
What are the reactions of
the society around gonna be?

00:41:08.771 --> 00:41:10.110
The way that black bodies are treated

00:41:10.110 --> 00:41:13.890
in each and every single,
you know, scenario,

00:41:13.890 --> 00:41:17.250
for the most part is different and almost

00:41:17.250 --> 00:41:20.430
always qualitatively worse, right?

00:41:20.430 --> 00:41:23.344
There are some times where
I show them stuff and

00:41:23.344 --> 00:41:25.084
they think it's white guilt

00:41:25.084 --> 00:41:26.490
and that not necessarily worse or better,

00:41:26.490 --> 00:41:27.630
but most of the time they think

00:41:27.630 --> 00:41:31.200
that it's qualitatively worse.

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:36.200
And so that's what I try to
sort of get across, right?

00:41:37.950 --> 00:41:40.740
And even in that one
specific video, right?

00:41:40.740 --> 00:41:44.250
It just debunks so many
stereotypes that white people

00:41:44.250 --> 00:41:46.293
are supposed to be scared of these like,

00:41:47.190 --> 00:41:50.010
wildin thugs or whatever, right?

00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:52.800
When in reality, it's
almost always the black body

00:41:52.800 --> 00:41:56.820
that's put in jeopardy by acting any way

00:41:56.820 --> 00:41:59.550
outside of the norm,
doing something deviant

00:41:59.550 --> 00:42:03.570
that a white kid is allowed individuality

00:42:03.570 --> 00:42:07.950
and not always confronted
about those things.

00:42:07.950 --> 00:42:12.363
That black bodies are often
in really vulnerable positions

00:42:15.090 --> 00:42:17.430
and often in really vulnerable positions

00:42:17.430 --> 00:42:20.610
where authority figures have the ability

00:42:20.610 --> 00:42:23.100
to do something to them, right?

00:42:23.100 --> 00:42:26.700
Suspend them from school, arrest them,

00:42:26.700 --> 00:42:30.033
put them in jail, right.
These sorts of things.

00:42:31.129 --> 00:42:36.030
And that just, cumulatively,
it adds up to so much, right?

00:42:36.030 --> 00:42:39.780
It adds up to stress, it adds
up to lower life expectancy.

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:44.610
It adds up to, you know,
building up all these stereotypes

00:42:44.610 --> 00:42:47.100
about black deviance and criminality

00:42:47.100 --> 00:42:50.280
when it's treated different
from the beginning.

00:42:50.280 --> 00:42:55.280
And so, yeah, I just, I
insert body so they can

00:42:56.798 --> 00:43:00.270
really understand and
empathize rather than

00:43:00.270 --> 00:43:02.220
just see a bunch of numbers.

00:43:02.220 --> 00:43:04.800
Like when I show the numbers
on the wealth disparity,

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:06.200
they don't wanna believe it.

00:43:07.140 --> 00:43:08.880
The wealth disparity is 10x,

00:43:08.880 --> 00:43:12.750
it's been 10x for 50, 60
years. I'll give 'em a quiz.

00:43:12.750 --> 00:43:14.580
They don't want to, they
just don't want to know.

00:43:14.580 --> 00:43:17.133
They'll still put two
times or four times, right.

00:43:18.630 --> 00:43:21.133
But when I actually am able to get them

00:43:21.133 --> 00:43:26.133
to visualize bodies or hypothetical
people it clicks better.

00:43:30.210 --> 00:43:31.043
- [Imo] Wow.

00:43:32.910 --> 00:43:33.743
Wow.

00:43:33.743 --> 00:43:35.250
- Yeah. I'm still taking that in.

00:43:35.250 --> 00:43:40.250
- Just get it wrong on the test
because I refuse to hear it.

00:43:44.127 --> 00:43:47.520
- And it's been very
consistent. That one question,

00:43:47.520 --> 00:43:49.770
especially across.

00:43:49.770 --> 00:43:52.560
- It's very telling.
It's very, very telling.

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:54.947
It says it all, it says it all.

00:43:56.834 --> 00:43:59.851
Okay. I, on my end, you know,

00:43:59.851 --> 00:44:01.290
I mean they're the courses I teach,

00:44:01.290 --> 00:44:03.963
but they're also the
special projects that I do.

00:44:04.980 --> 00:44:08.190
And just by way of this
conversation we're having

00:44:08.190 --> 00:44:10.890
about black bodies,
there was one that I did

00:44:10.890 --> 00:44:12.840
at Westfield State University,
which is, like I said,

00:44:12.840 --> 00:44:14.520
a predominantly white school.

00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:15.723
And I should tell you,

00:44:16.860 --> 00:44:19.080
here's how I arrived at this project.

00:44:19.080 --> 00:44:22.140
It's a project titled "17 Years Boy."

00:44:22.140 --> 00:44:26.280
And I still don't know
what that project was,

00:44:26.280 --> 00:44:29.220
and I'm glad it's over
to be honest with you.

00:44:29.220 --> 00:44:34.220
But before that project started,
here's what had happened.

00:44:34.590 --> 00:44:35.670
Alright?

00:44:35.670 --> 00:44:38.523
Donald Trump had recently
been inaugurated as president.

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:41.010
Determined at another time,

00:44:41.010 --> 00:44:44.013
if any of the following
things were connected to him,

00:44:45.180 --> 00:44:49.260
there was this huge
Charlottesville incident.

00:44:49.260 --> 00:44:51.840
It was brought upon by riots,

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:54.840
by white nationalists
and white supremacists.

00:44:54.840 --> 00:44:57.960
And there was a whole lot of wickedness

00:44:57.960 --> 00:44:59.733
that looked white there, okay.

00:45:01.230 --> 00:45:04.680
In Charlottesville. And people died.

00:45:04.680 --> 00:45:07.773
One person died, two cops
died, three people died.

00:45:09.330 --> 00:45:10.533
So that had happened.

00:45:11.760 --> 00:45:14.700
Someone in my church, the
church I no longer attend,

00:45:14.700 --> 00:45:17.523
predominantly white church,
white evangelical church,

00:45:18.870 --> 00:45:23.163
had posted news to his
Facebook page, public,

00:45:24.450 --> 00:45:27.420
he says, in response
to Black Lives Matters.

00:45:27.420 --> 00:45:30.030
And all of a sudden, because
treason, they'll say,

00:45:30.030 --> 00:45:32.070
you know, they're... you know!

00:45:32.070 --> 00:45:36.570
No, no, not lynching,
traditional way of, you know,

00:45:36.570 --> 00:45:40.110
treasonous would be,
so it's news, you know?

00:45:40.110 --> 00:45:42.090
Well, yeah, we know the history
of black people with news,

00:45:42.090 --> 00:45:43.290
but that's not what I meant.

00:45:43.290 --> 00:45:45.240
So he posted that to his Facebook page.

00:45:46.800 --> 00:45:47.840
I was...

00:45:49.740 --> 00:45:50.760
I won't go into that story,

00:45:50.760 --> 00:45:52.290
but a number of things happened

00:45:52.290 --> 00:45:54.273
and didn't happen as a result of that.

00:45:55.500 --> 00:46:00.183
And then school starts, and
two weeks into school starting,

00:46:04.410 --> 00:46:08.790
black students are accosted
with racist language

00:46:08.790 --> 00:46:12.540
written on their doors, "Nigger go home."

00:46:12.540 --> 00:46:14.580
Or, "Niggers live here,"
or whatever it was.

00:46:14.580 --> 00:46:16.380
Sounded very, very sixties.

00:46:16.380 --> 00:46:17.700
It's really interesting how the language

00:46:17.700 --> 00:46:22.020
hasn't changed much. So, you know, and so,

00:46:22.020 --> 00:46:23.670
all of these things are happening

00:46:25.920 --> 00:46:30.920
and there are people planning marches

00:46:31.050 --> 00:46:33.600
because of the racist
incidents at Westfield State.

00:46:34.501 --> 00:46:37.920
There were rape threats to
black girls that were there.

00:46:37.920 --> 00:46:39.630
All kinds of things were happening.

00:46:39.630 --> 00:46:41.310
That semester in particular was hot,

00:46:41.310 --> 00:46:42.960
but it wasn't just at Westfield State.

00:46:42.960 --> 00:46:45.660
It was happening around
the United States, okay.

00:46:45.660 --> 00:46:47.640
At a number of different institutions.

00:46:47.640 --> 00:46:52.140
And so I decided to have a
project that would look at

00:46:52.140 --> 00:46:54.453
the life and death of Trayvon Martin,

00:46:55.860 --> 00:46:57.943
because people were asking me,

00:46:57.943 --> 00:46:59.250
are you gonna be at this protest?
Are you gonna be at this?

00:46:59.250 --> 00:47:01.020
I'm like, everybody leave me alone.

00:47:01.020 --> 00:47:02.550
You all go ahead and do your marching,

00:47:02.550 --> 00:47:05.220
whatever's gonna make you feel better.

00:47:05.220 --> 00:47:08.460
But I'm gonna do what
artists do. We make things.

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:10.260
And so I brought together,

00:47:10.260 --> 00:47:13.240
it was a collaborative
project that involved

00:47:15.733 --> 00:47:19.410
the 17 hour long, it took
four days to do painting

00:47:19.410 --> 00:47:20.880
of a portrait of Trayvon Martin

00:47:20.880 --> 00:47:23.010
that was destroyed at the 17th hour.

00:47:23.010 --> 00:47:24.737
Nobody knew it was gonna be destroyed.

00:47:24.737 --> 00:47:29.737
It was an extremely emotional work of art.

00:47:29.910 --> 00:47:32.100
And many people are still
wondering what happened.

00:47:32.100 --> 00:47:33.987
People were asking, well,
why is he doing this?

00:47:33.987 --> 00:47:35.760
The people who were
criticizing it were like,

00:47:35.760 --> 00:47:36.909
this doesn't make any sense.

00:47:36.909 --> 00:47:39.000
That was, like six years ago.

00:47:39.000 --> 00:47:41.850
But it was weird in the
room that I constructed,

00:47:41.850 --> 00:47:44.070
you could hear the sounds
of the Zimmerman trial,

00:47:44.070 --> 00:47:45.510
like that was playing.

00:47:45.510 --> 00:47:47.940
So it was like you were
walking into a time warp

00:47:47.940 --> 00:47:49.710
where we were six years prior.

00:47:49.710 --> 00:47:52.410
We were all living in
that moment where this boy

00:47:52.410 --> 00:47:54.573
who was brutalized,

00:47:58.646 --> 00:48:00.840
was dragged through the Zimmerman trial,

00:48:00.840 --> 00:48:02.190
it became the Trayvon Martin trial.

00:48:02.190 --> 00:48:04.380
We all lived through that about how

00:48:04.380 --> 00:48:07.230
he could be killed twice, you know?

00:48:07.230 --> 00:48:11.670
And so there was this
conversation about black bodies

00:48:11.670 --> 00:48:15.120
and the things that happened
to us and why, you know,

00:48:15.120 --> 00:48:18.120
graffiti on doors such
as, "Nigger go home,"

00:48:18.120 --> 00:48:23.120
or, "Niggers live here,"
in then, 2017, 2018.

00:48:23.370 --> 00:48:26.370
Really, you can draw a line to it,

00:48:26.370 --> 00:48:30.000
and it's a national one
and it's a local one.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:32.940
And it confused a lot of people.

00:48:32.940 --> 00:48:36.390
A lot of people felt good
that it was happening as well.

00:48:36.390 --> 00:48:39.510
And I was completely
devastated when a very,

00:48:39.510 --> 00:48:41.700
very high ranking person at the school,

00:48:41.700 --> 00:48:44.531
when it was all done, came and said,

00:48:44.531 --> 00:48:46.470
this has just been amazing.

00:48:46.470 --> 00:48:48.210
When are you gonna do it again?

00:48:48.210 --> 00:48:49.043
And I...

00:48:52.530 --> 00:48:54.720
I looked at him.

00:48:54.720 --> 00:48:56.310
Here we had put together art,

00:48:56.310 --> 00:48:57.570
the music department had come together,

00:48:57.570 --> 00:48:59.790
the English department, there were songs.

00:48:59.790 --> 00:49:01.990
It was a very emotional thing

00:49:03.450 --> 00:49:05.220
in commemoration of Trayvon Martin,

00:49:05.220 --> 00:49:10.080
but also of 17 other boys
over the past century,

00:49:10.080 --> 00:49:13.740
starting from 1915 who had
had things like this done.

00:49:13.740 --> 00:49:16.563
So it was like, a shrine.

00:49:18.000 --> 00:49:20.670
And this man came to me
and said, please, Imo,

00:49:20.670 --> 00:49:21.630
we need to do more of this.

00:49:21.630 --> 00:49:22.650
When are you gonna do this again?

00:49:22.650 --> 00:49:24.586
And I was like, I don't know. Let's see.

00:49:24.586 --> 00:49:26.929
The next boy's gonna be killed next week.

00:49:26.929 --> 00:49:28.740
Let's put it together again.

00:49:28.740 --> 00:49:33.090
It was very odd and
missing the mark, you know?

00:49:33.090 --> 00:49:35.490
And so there is this way that

00:49:35.490 --> 00:49:40.320
I can imagine that Danny
has poured himself out

00:49:40.320 --> 00:49:43.720
into a lecture or into a
work of a piece of writing

00:49:44.580 --> 00:49:49.580
or into a talk only to
have the point just.

00:49:51.801 --> 00:49:53.820
- Oh yeah.
- Just gone.

00:49:53.820 --> 00:49:56.940
And you wonder, are you
shouting into a void

00:49:56.940 --> 00:49:59.700
that just consumes what
it is you're saying?

00:49:59.700 --> 00:50:02.670
Is there any way that change happens?

00:50:02.670 --> 00:50:07.650
Is what I'm doing, does
it actually move the...

00:50:07.650 --> 00:50:09.063
Does it move anything?

00:50:10.207 --> 00:50:12.600
You know? And that's
something that I wonder often.

00:50:12.600 --> 00:50:13.770
I mean, I'm still, when I get off,

00:50:13.770 --> 00:50:15.600
when I'm done with you guys, I'm going,

00:50:15.600 --> 00:50:16.740
I'm working on the next thing

00:50:16.740 --> 00:50:19.380
because the conversation has to continue.

00:50:19.380 --> 00:50:23.490
But there is this fear
sometimes that people are seeing

00:50:23.490 --> 00:50:26.973
the artistry and missing
the point. That worries me.

00:50:27.990 --> 00:50:29.520
- Absolutely.

00:50:29.520 --> 00:50:33.117
I've had a number of
instances like that, and yeah,

00:50:35.460 --> 00:50:37.713
it is tough to deal with.

00:50:39.030 --> 00:50:44.030
I would say that that's
where individual emails

00:50:44.460 --> 00:50:46.890
or something like maybe
a text from a student,

00:50:46.890 --> 00:50:51.150
even if it's a couple weeks
later be like, I get it now,

00:50:51.150 --> 00:50:56.150
are really valuable resources
to, you know, keep you going.

00:50:58.650 --> 00:50:59.850
- Yep.

00:50:59.850 --> 00:51:02.340
- Yeah. That's, I mean, it's sobering,

00:51:02.340 --> 00:51:04.440
but like those little
pieces, those little things,

00:51:04.440 --> 00:51:06.240
like I said, make a huge difference.

00:51:09.510 --> 00:51:12.510
Well, one thing just to, as
a last pivot, I'm curious,

00:51:12.510 --> 00:51:13.343
and now I'm really curious,

00:51:13.343 --> 00:51:14.850
just the way this conversation's
gone, I'm curious,

00:51:14.850 --> 00:51:17.340
what keeps you all going?

00:51:17.340 --> 00:51:19.080
Like, what gives you hope
in what you're doing?

00:51:19.080 --> 00:51:20.070
Because I mean, we've
talked about, I mean,

00:51:20.070 --> 00:51:23.400
there's obviously the passion
for the craft that you have,

00:51:23.400 --> 00:51:25.470
but there's also the challenges
and you've done a nice job

00:51:25.470 --> 00:51:27.960
of kind of laying out
that reality. But just,

00:51:27.960 --> 00:51:29.250
I'm sure our listeners
are curious too, like,

00:51:29.250 --> 00:51:31.290
why are you all still doing this work?

00:51:31.290 --> 00:51:32.550
What keeps you moving?

00:51:32.550 --> 00:51:35.310
Besides those texts and those messages?

00:51:35.310 --> 00:51:37.020
Dan, do you wanna start?

00:51:37.020 --> 00:51:40.173
- It's not the huge
academia checks either.

00:51:41.551 --> 00:51:44.634
(everybody laughing)

00:51:46.290 --> 00:51:51.290
- The collective laughter,
of course, oh gosh.

00:51:51.600 --> 00:51:54.240
- We're relatively okay,
but we're not buying

00:51:54.240 --> 00:51:56.643
any NFL franchise anytime.

00:51:59.880 --> 00:52:03.900
So there are like real
world tangible things

00:52:03.900 --> 00:52:05.580
that give me hope, right?

00:52:05.580 --> 00:52:09.420
I've seen, since I've started
doing my work with atheists,

00:52:09.420 --> 00:52:11.880
specifically black atheists,

00:52:11.880 --> 00:52:15.387
there has been some
stigma lifted around that.

00:52:15.387 --> 00:52:18.330
And that, especially
within the black community,

00:52:18.330 --> 00:52:20.940
I've seen some of that stigma lifted.

00:52:20.940 --> 00:52:23.880
And I think even, at least as importantly,

00:52:23.880 --> 00:52:27.993
some of that more secular public figure,

00:52:28.860 --> 00:52:30.333
black history reclaimed.

00:52:34.080 --> 00:52:36.330
In terms of seeing more black faculty

00:52:36.330 --> 00:52:41.330
in certain institutions really
trying to live up to ideals,

00:52:41.520 --> 00:52:44.970
I do have hope from those things.

00:52:44.970 --> 00:52:47.973
And so I try to lean into that,

00:52:50.007 --> 00:52:51.930
that there is hope to fix the things

00:52:51.930 --> 00:52:53.580
that have been really slow, right?

00:52:53.580 --> 00:52:58.580
Like actual fixing or
narrowing of racial disparities

00:53:00.240 --> 00:53:03.900
that has been slow going,
if no going at all, right?

00:53:03.900 --> 00:53:06.450
The world disparity, home ownership gap,

00:53:06.450 --> 00:53:10.923
those things are bigger than
when Dr. King spoke in 1963.

00:53:13.800 --> 00:53:17.880
But the progress that I see
on the interpersonal side,

00:53:17.880 --> 00:53:20.490
again, lends me to just
want to keep chopping

00:53:20.490 --> 00:53:25.490
and hope that we can have
enough cultural understandings

00:53:26.310 --> 00:53:30.570
and eventually some paradigm
shifts that lead to places

00:53:30.570 --> 00:53:33.540
where we start to see
outcomes being more equal.

00:53:33.540 --> 00:53:37.350
We start to see the qualitative treatment

00:53:37.350 --> 00:53:39.810
of different groups being much more equal,

00:53:39.810 --> 00:53:41.043
much less disparate.

00:53:42.600 --> 00:53:45.723
And so that's really the
world that I'm hoping for.

00:53:45.723 --> 00:53:47.580
I think that it's achievable.

00:53:47.580 --> 00:53:50.403
I've seen individuals make the change.

00:53:52.560 --> 00:53:54.720
So that's what gives me
hope. The thing that,

00:53:54.720 --> 00:53:58.027
you know, scares me is just

00:53:59.970 --> 00:54:02.703
how incentivized racism has become,

00:54:03.600 --> 00:54:05.403
on the right wing especially.

00:54:06.900 --> 00:54:11.900
And that is a very strong competing force

00:54:12.780 --> 00:54:14.223
to what I want to see.

00:54:15.570 --> 00:54:18.690
And it has a lot of money
and it has a lot of power

00:54:18.690 --> 00:54:22.740
and it has a lot of
structural things going for it

00:54:22.740 --> 00:54:25.350
in terms of minoritarian
government prevailing

00:54:25.350 --> 00:54:26.853
quite a bit in this country.

00:54:28.650 --> 00:54:29.793
And so, yeah.

00:54:31.260 --> 00:54:32.880
I hope that makes sense. Right?

00:54:32.880 --> 00:54:36.720
I've got to overcome that
obstacle because of the

00:54:36.720 --> 00:54:40.923
things that I've seen
within people, especially.

00:54:42.060 --> 00:54:44.790
Again, some institutions,
because the institutions

00:54:44.790 --> 00:54:48.450
have been very slow to
change in this society.

00:54:48.450 --> 00:54:50.850
- Yeah. And I definitely
appreciate that perspective.

00:54:50.850 --> 00:54:52.470
I mean, as I'm reflecting
on what you're saying,

00:54:52.470 --> 00:54:54.270
it actually reminds me of the
story you were talking about

00:54:54.270 --> 00:54:56.640
with the wealth gap and
how you just, you know,

00:54:56.640 --> 00:54:59.370
you're continuing, 'cause
that's in a microcosm.

00:54:59.370 --> 00:55:00.960
The students might not
be seeing it at first.

00:55:00.960 --> 00:55:03.390
You package it in a different
way. Eventually they catch it.

00:55:03.390 --> 00:55:05.742
And just hearing you talk about
that on the societal level

00:55:05.742 --> 00:55:09.330
in terms of stigma for black
atheists or other types of

00:55:09.330 --> 00:55:11.760
things that are moving
at the individual level

00:55:11.760 --> 00:55:15.257
institutionally, that gives
me hope hearing you say that.

00:55:15.257 --> 00:55:19.020
'Cause I know that's a
tension that I feel often,

00:55:19.020 --> 00:55:21.300
especially I know even in
this podcast, like, okay,

00:55:21.300 --> 00:55:22.410
well what am I doing in the lab?

00:55:22.410 --> 00:55:23.280
What am I doing with students?

00:55:23.280 --> 00:55:24.690
Is it making a little difference here?

00:55:24.690 --> 00:55:26.673
Is it making a difference society wide?

00:55:27.508 --> 00:55:30.660
And it almost sounds
too selfish in a sense.

00:55:30.660 --> 00:55:32.040
'Cause it's almost as if I'm saying,

00:55:32.040 --> 00:55:34.230
where am I feeling like I'm
actually making an impact

00:55:34.230 --> 00:55:37.410
in someone's life or in
someone's lives or in society?

00:55:37.410 --> 00:55:39.480
So to kind of hear you package that for me

00:55:39.480 --> 00:55:40.920
is also just really helpful.

00:55:40.920 --> 00:55:45.330
- That reminds me of something
I said to my wife when,

00:55:45.330 --> 00:55:46.710
about two or three weeks ago,

00:55:46.710 --> 00:55:50.190
there was the fallout of
Beyonce not winning the Grammy.

00:55:50.190 --> 00:55:51.720
I don't keep up with the award shows,

00:55:51.720 --> 00:55:53.550
I think it was a Grammy.

00:55:53.550 --> 00:55:56.550
And I said to her, well, I don't,

00:55:56.550 --> 00:55:58.320
I didn't listen to that
album, but she did.

00:55:58.320 --> 00:56:00.543
I heard it in the back. It was okay.

00:56:01.410 --> 00:56:03.600
But I said to her 30 years ago,

00:56:03.600 --> 00:56:06.990
we wouldn't have even had
a public discussion about

00:56:06.990 --> 00:56:09.900
should a black woman have won a Grammy.

00:56:09.900 --> 00:56:12.870
So we need to acknowledge what's racist,

00:56:12.870 --> 00:56:15.360
what kind of bias they're
going on over there.

00:56:15.360 --> 00:56:18.583
But also that there's
hope because we are in

00:56:18.583 --> 00:56:21.180
a different space than we we used to be.

00:56:21.180 --> 00:56:22.597
- Yeah, that's true.

00:56:22.597 --> 00:56:23.453
That's good perspective.

00:56:24.510 --> 00:56:26.070
Imo, I'm gonna give you the last word.

00:56:26.070 --> 00:56:31.070
- My hope is in the relationships
that I'm forming with,

00:56:31.590 --> 00:56:35.190
I think some of the most
extraordinary people

00:56:35.190 --> 00:56:40.190
as I create and as I deal with
the various themes, you know,

00:56:43.640 --> 00:56:46.980
in my work as an artist, as a professor,

00:56:46.980 --> 00:56:49.140
and just as a human being.

00:56:49.140 --> 00:56:52.263
So the relationships bring hope for me.

00:56:53.160 --> 00:56:55.560
I'm not working in a vacuum.
Things are happening.

00:56:55.560 --> 00:56:56.853
The world is moving.

00:56:59.760 --> 00:57:04.760
And what I do as an artist
is just another offering.

00:57:07.950 --> 00:57:12.660
It's another offering and an
opening for a conversation.

00:57:12.660 --> 00:57:17.660
I sometimes find it easier to
deal with my emotions around

00:57:20.490 --> 00:57:25.490
a series of things, you know,
through the avenue of art.

00:57:26.720 --> 00:57:28.503
And what I like about it is that,

00:57:29.487 --> 00:57:30.540
I don't always know what I'm doing.

00:57:30.540 --> 00:57:32.910
In fact, most of the time I don't.

00:57:32.910 --> 00:57:35.490
I don't do sketches before
I do the final work.

00:57:35.490 --> 00:57:39.903
I just, it comes out as a
unit, that's how I work.

00:57:41.079 --> 00:57:43.890
And it's pretty honest when it does,

00:57:43.890 --> 00:57:47.560
it's perhaps more honest.
I'm more honest in line

00:57:49.455 --> 00:57:51.450
than I am when I'm speaking.

00:57:51.450 --> 00:57:56.190
And there's something
childlike about making things,

00:57:56.190 --> 00:57:58.173
starting at nothing and then making it.

00:57:59.130 --> 00:58:01.260
There's something a little
narcissistic about it.

00:58:01.260 --> 00:58:04.770
I'm deciding that that
is going to be something

00:58:04.770 --> 00:58:08.730
when anybody looking at it
can say there's nothing there.

00:58:08.730 --> 00:58:11.040
And so somewhere in that tension between

00:58:11.040 --> 00:58:15.660
a God complex and a
child is the opportunity

00:58:15.660 --> 00:58:19.290
for a new kind of conversation to start.

00:58:19.290 --> 00:58:24.290
And because God is
always making new things,

00:58:26.580 --> 00:58:28.020
I just don't know what
it's gonna look like.

00:58:28.020 --> 00:58:29.640
I don't know what it's gonna do.

00:58:29.640 --> 00:58:33.270
And there's hope in not knowing
if that makes any sense.

00:58:33.270 --> 00:58:35.793
Like, we think that, you know,

00:58:37.140 --> 00:58:39.930
we think we know what the
next five minutes will bring.

00:58:39.930 --> 00:58:41.940
And the fact is we don't.

00:58:41.940 --> 00:58:46.023
There's somewhere in
there that gives me hope,

00:58:47.820 --> 00:58:50.470
that something really
extraordinary could take place.

00:58:51.840 --> 00:58:55.230
I think it's child-like,
but I'm okay with that.

00:58:55.230 --> 00:58:58.443
In terms of being a
teacher or a professor,

00:58:59.580 --> 00:59:01.503
it's still in the relationships.

00:59:03.150 --> 00:59:04.860
When I teach my art survey courses,

00:59:04.860 --> 00:59:07.983
it's a general course in art history.

00:59:09.360 --> 00:59:11.823
I always start with
Africa. I have my reasons.

00:59:12.810 --> 00:59:16.830
I've never been a part
of an art survey course

00:59:16.830 --> 00:59:19.980
in my training that has
started with Africa.

00:59:19.980 --> 00:59:24.980
I start with Africa and we
go through it and I have,

00:59:25.980 --> 00:59:29.670
in one of my classes,
I have one black kid,

00:59:29.670 --> 00:59:31.440
two black students.

00:59:31.440 --> 00:59:34.500
And this one guy, he's just sharp.

00:59:34.500 --> 00:59:38.100
He's a brilliant young
man. He's a first year.

00:59:38.100 --> 00:59:40.620
And I was talking about
something I forgot.

00:59:40.620 --> 00:59:43.660
It was a video showing of
some ritual or a ceremony and

00:59:43.660 --> 00:59:46.620
there were women dancing
and I was talking,

00:59:46.620 --> 00:59:48.570
I was trying to stay focused,

00:59:48.570 --> 00:59:51.510
but I panned the room as I
was giving this impassioned

00:59:51.510 --> 00:59:56.510
end of the lecture and
the smile on his face.

00:59:57.030 --> 01:00:00.363
He was just looking at the
screen. It looked cartoonish.

01:00:01.354 --> 01:00:05.310
He was beaming ear to ear,

01:00:05.310 --> 01:00:08.040
and he waited till after
class to meet with me,

01:00:08.040 --> 01:00:10.410
and he's like, I don't know how to.

01:00:10.410 --> 01:00:12.960
Look. I'm African-American.

01:00:12.960 --> 01:00:15.660
You know, that's Africa.

01:00:15.660 --> 01:00:18.060
There's gotta be some
kind of genetic memory.

01:00:18.060 --> 01:00:21.863
I know those women, I
know how they're moving.

01:00:21.863 --> 01:00:25.500
I get that. Like I feel
like I'm related to them.

01:00:25.500 --> 01:00:26.460
So he was explaining,

01:00:26.460 --> 01:00:29.337
he didn't know that I saw
this big grin on his face.

01:00:29.337 --> 01:00:32.880
But he was explaining how he
was feeling in that moment.

01:00:32.880 --> 01:00:35.793
And I'm like, I'm supposed to
be at this school right now.

01:00:37.620 --> 01:00:39.150
He needs this.

01:00:39.150 --> 01:00:43.200
And there's students like
him that need this right now.

01:00:43.200 --> 01:00:44.610
And so there's hope in that for me.

01:00:44.610 --> 01:00:46.620
There's hope that I'm
in the right position

01:00:46.620 --> 01:00:48.603
at the right place and time.

01:00:50.046 --> 01:00:55.046
That my being in this particular
moment means something

01:00:55.350 --> 01:00:57.843
to the people who are gathered around me.

01:00:58.887 --> 01:01:01.323
And I'm okay with just that.

01:01:02.620 --> 01:01:05.280
- It's a great juxtaposition
from the undergrad,

01:01:05.280 --> 01:01:10.020
not feeling like you sould
be here to that moment.

01:01:10.020 --> 01:01:12.990
Giving you the exact opposite feeling

01:01:12.990 --> 01:01:16.320
of I should be here.
And this is exactly why.

01:01:16.320 --> 01:01:18.060
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:01:18.060 --> 01:01:19.710
- It's awesome.

01:01:19.710 --> 01:01:22.170
- Great illustration. Wow.

01:01:22.170 --> 01:01:23.820
Well, I'm grateful of both of you, one,

01:01:23.820 --> 01:01:26.640
for just the work that you
all do on a daily basis.

01:01:26.640 --> 01:01:29.460
I mean, even, not despite,

01:01:29.460 --> 01:01:31.470
but amidst everything that
you all navigate just to

01:01:31.470 --> 01:01:33.727
even to get to where you
are as you all shared so,

01:01:33.727 --> 01:01:35.460
so powerfully and eloquently.

01:01:35.460 --> 01:01:38.160
And then just the impact it's
having on that generation.

01:01:38.160 --> 01:01:39.780
Definitely full circle as Danny mentioned.

01:01:39.780 --> 01:01:42.120
So thank you to both of you for the work.

01:01:42.120 --> 01:01:44.310
Thank you for taking time outta
your schedules to actually

01:01:44.310 --> 01:01:45.510
be here to share this.

01:01:45.510 --> 01:01:47.460
I know listeners are gonna
get a lot out of this.

01:01:47.460 --> 01:01:49.410
I'm just anticipating there's
so many things that people are

01:01:49.410 --> 01:01:51.870
gonna be able to relate to
or have their eyes open to

01:01:51.870 --> 01:01:53.880
at the same time, or a little bit of both.

01:01:53.880 --> 01:01:55.470
So definitely appreciate
what you're all doing.

01:01:55.470 --> 01:01:57.510
Hopefully this will give
other people hope as well

01:01:57.510 --> 01:02:00.030
as we all try and move
forward as a society.

01:02:00.030 --> 01:02:02.070
So Danny, Imo, thanks
so much for being here.

01:02:02.070 --> 01:02:03.720
Definitely appreciate both of you.

01:02:03.720 --> 01:02:04.590
- Awesome. Thank you.

01:02:04.590 --> 01:02:05.513
I had a blast. This was...

01:02:05.513 --> 01:02:06.873
- I had a blast too.

